"<p>A managed server can publish its apps through a <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/connections/connect-networks/\">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> instead of exposing public ports <code>80</code> and <code>443</code>. Nitrobase only requires the tunnel connector token—it never calls the Cloudflare API or stores Cloudflare account credentials.</p>\n<h2>Setup Steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In the <strong>Cloudflare Zero Trust</strong> dashboard, go to <strong>Networks → Tunnels</strong>, create a remotely managed tunnel (<code>cloudflared</code>), and copy its connector token.</li>\n<li>In Nitrobase, open <strong>Server → Settings → Cloudflare Tunnel</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Paste the connector token (stored in <code>/etc/nitrobase/tunnel/cloudflared.env</code> with <code>0600</code> permissions and encrypted in PostgreSQL).</li>\n<li>Enable the tunnel.</li>\n<li>Optionally enable <strong>Tunnel only</strong> mode so Traefik does not bind public ports <code>80</code>/<code>443</code> on the host firewall.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In Cloudflare Zero Trust, add a <strong>Public Hostname</strong> for each domain pointing to the service:\n<pre class=\"shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark\" style=\"--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e\" tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"language-text\"><span class=\"line\"><span>http://nitrobase-traefik:8081</span></span></code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>In Nitrobase, mark the domain as <strong>Via Cloudflare Tunnel</strong> in the app's <strong>Domains</strong> tab.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Network Isolation Architecture</h2>\n<p>Nitrobase isolates tunnel traffic on a dedicated private Docker network (<code>nitrobase-tunnel</code>, default subnet <code>172.31.254.0/29</code>) shared <strong>only</strong> by <code>nitrobase-traefik</code> (bound to <code>172.31.254.2:8081</code>) and <code>nitrobase-cloudflared</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>App containers on <code>nitrobase-network</code> cannot reach the tunnel entrypoint.</li>\n<li>Tunnel-routed domains are attached exclusively to the private <code>:8081</code> entrypoint, so nobody can bypass Cloudflare Access or WAF by sending requests directly to the server's public IP on port <code>80</code>/<code>443</code>.</li>\n<li>Nitrobase probes <code>cloudflared</code>'s internal <code>/ready</code> metrics endpoint (<code>CLOUDFLARED_METRICS_PORT</code>, default <code>20241</code>) to report live tunnel connector status.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"docCallout docCallout-tip\"><div class=\"docCalloutHeader\"><span class=\"docCalloutBadge\">TIP</span><span class=\"docCalloutTitle\">Zero Open Web Ports</span></div><div class=\"docCalloutContent\">\n<p>With <strong>Tunnel only</strong> enabled, you can block ports <code>80</code> and <code>443</code> completely in your VPS firewall—only outbound connections from <code>cloudflared</code> are needed.</p>\n</div></div>"